[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1,pc2,pc3
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Client resolver configuration (search domain/suffix). The campus DNS server itself is up and authoritative for jeremysitlab.com.

Mechanism: Users are issuing short, unqualified names (e.g., “srv1”). All clients tested lack a search domain, so resolvers do not expand “srv1” to “srv1.jeremysitlab.com” and the query returns no answer. This appears to users as “DNS is down.” Fully qualified names under jeremysitlab.com resolve normally.

Localization: On SRV1 (10.5.0.4), the interface is up (10.5.0.4/24) and the DNS service is answering: dig @10.5.0.4 jeremysitlab.com SOA returns NOERROR and is authoritative; netstat shows port 53 listening on 10.5.0.4; the zone file /etc/bind/db.jeremysitlab is present and contains records (including wildcard). From a client (pc3), getent hosts srv1.jeremysitlab.com resolves to 10.5.0.4 and ping succeeds. On all clients (pc1, pc2, pc3), /etc/resolv.conf contains only “nameserver 10.5.0.4” with no “search jeremysitlab.com,” and “dig srv1” returns no data, confirming the suffix omission.

Root cause: Client-side resolver search domain missing (likely DHCP didn’t supply domain-search or local config drift), not a SRV1 failure.

Corrective fix: Do not restart SRV1. Add “search jeremysitlab.com” to client resolv.conf via DHCP option (domain-name or domain-search) or manual config, then restart the client network stack (e.g., systemctl restart NetworkManager or dhclient) on affected PCs (pc1, pc2, pc3). After this, unqualified names (e.g., “srv1”) will resolve correctly.
=== END ===